WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.880 --> 00:00:03.400
When it comes to something
as life-and-death important

00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.120
as what to feed ourselves
and our families,

00:00:06.120 --> 00:00:10.760
we should rely not on
anecdotes, but on facts.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.040
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast.

00:00:13.040 --> 00:00:16.960
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:16.960 --> 00:00:21.080
Today we continue with our
series on exercise as medicine.

00:00:21.080 --> 00:00:22.120
And I have to say,

00:00:22.120 --> 00:00:26.320
I was surprised by how much controversy
there is in the medical literature

00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:31.760
over whether the apparent longevity
benefits of exercise are even real.

00:00:31.760 --> 00:00:34.120
Whether six percent
of premature mortality

00:00:34.120 --> 00:00:37.560
is related to physical
inactivity, nine percent,

00:00:37.560 --> 00:00:40.520
or even 15 percent,
these estimates

00:00:40.520 --> 00:00:43.440
are all predicated
on the presumption that

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:46.760
the associations found linking
inactivity to death rates

00:00:46.760 --> 00:00:50.612
in observational studies
constitute cause-and-effect.

00:00:50.760 --> 00:00:55.440
A classic study from the 1950's
entitled "Coronary Heart-Disease

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.800
and Physical Activity of Work"
illustrates how difficult

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:02.960
it can be to tease out causality
between physical activity

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:04.600
and longevity.

00:01:04.600 --> 00:01:08.240
London bus drivers appeared
to have twice the risk of dying

00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:11.520
from heart disease
compared to bus conductors

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:13.200
who were presumed
to be protected

00:01:13.200 --> 00:01:16.040
by their climbing up
and down 500 to 700

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:19.280
steps a day
in the famous double deckers.

00:01:19.280 --> 00:01:22.040
However, it was later revealed
in a follow up paper

00:01:22.040 --> 00:01:26.160
"Physique of London Busmen,"
that based on the measurements

00:01:26.160 --> 00:01:30.160
of their starting uniforms,
the bus drivers started out

00:01:30.160 --> 00:01:32.400
significantly heavier.

00:01:32.400 --> 00:01:35.600
Similar issues surrounding
reverse causality continue

00:01:35.600 --> 00:01:39.880
to haunt observational exercise
studies to this day.

00:01:39.880 --> 00:01:43.200
Is exercising enabling
good health in seniors,

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.360
or is good health
enabling exercise

00:01:45.360 --> 00:01:46.840
in the first place?

00:01:46.840 --> 00:01:48.960
Is inactivity leading
to chronic disease,

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:52.440
or is chronic disease
leading to inactivity?

00:01:52.440 --> 00:01:55.160
Then there are
the confounding factors.

00:01:55.160 --> 00:01:58.120
The archetypical one
being less smoking

00:01:58.120 --> 00:01:59.840
among active individuals.

00:02:00.360 --> 00:02:03.320
With the exception
of elite male power athletes

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:07.160
who may die up to nearly
five times the normal rate

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.640
(perhaps due to use
of anabolic steroids),

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:11.080
athletes tend to live longer

00:02:11.080 --> 00:02:12.960
than their
sedentary counterparts,

00:02:12.960 --> 00:02:15.760
as much as four
to eight years longer.

00:02:15.760 --> 00:02:18.120
But think of all
the complicating elements,

00:02:18.120 --> 00:02:20.200
especially among professionals.

00:02:20.200 --> 00:02:24.920
Major league baseball players,
Tour de France cyclists,

00:02:24.920 --> 00:02:27.800
and champion skiers
all live longer

00:02:27.800 --> 00:02:29.920
than the general population.

00:02:29.920 --> 00:02:31.920
But that doesn't mean
that necessarily has anything

00:02:31.920 --> 00:02:37.400
to do with home runs,
bicyclettes, or the slopes.

00:02:37.400 --> 00:02:40.920
Maybe it's partly the superior
genetic constitution

00:02:40.920 --> 00:02:42.920
that allow
for such physical feats

00:02:42.920 --> 00:02:46.240
in the first place
or the socioeconomic status

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:48.400
bestowed upon the winner.

00:02:48.400 --> 00:02:50.920
Yes, Olympic medalists
live longer

00:02:50.920 --> 00:02:56.000
than the general population,
but so do grandmasters of chess,

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:00.080
as revealed by a study subtitled
"Mind versus muscle," suggesting

00:03:00.080 --> 00:03:04.960
it's the afforded status
rather than muscular exertion.

00:03:04.960 --> 00:03:08.120
Winners of a Nobel Prize
or an Oscar award

00:03:08.120 --> 00:03:12.440
similarly have
superior life expectancy.

00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:15.120
To try to tease out
these factors,

00:03:15.120 --> 00:03:16.840
researchers have compared
the effects

00:03:16.840 --> 00:03:18.840
of leisure-time
physical activity

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.240
to occupational
physical activity.

00:03:22.240 --> 00:03:26.320
If the link between exercise
and longevity were truly causal,

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:29.920
then the context within which
you get the same exertion

00:03:29.920 --> 00:03:31.600
shouldn't matter.

00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:34.160
As you can probably guess,
though, manual labor

00:03:34.160 --> 00:03:36.400
can sometimes be associated
with a shorter,

00:03:36.400 --> 00:03:39.440
not longer, life,
again suggesting the primacy

00:03:39.440 --> 00:03:43.280
of confounders
like socioeconomic factors.

00:03:43.280 --> 00:03:46.440
Is it possible
that a genetic predisposition

00:03:46.440 --> 00:03:48.760
to physical fitness
is what accounts

00:03:48.760 --> 00:03:52.120
for the exercise-longevity link
rather than

00:03:52.120 --> 00:03:53.880
the physical activity itself?

00:03:53.880 --> 00:03:58.120
In other words, is it
just genetic selection bias?

00:03:58.120 --> 00:04:02.280
We know exercise and longevity
are associated with one another,

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:06.440
but instead of exercise causing
the longevity,

00:04:06.440 --> 00:04:08.880
maybe the same
genetic predisposition

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:12.640
to exercise also leads
to longevity,

00:04:12.640 --> 00:04:16.160
whether or not
you actually exercise at all.

00:04:16.160 --> 00:04:22.120
This question was raised by
a pair of mind-blowing studies.

00:04:22.120 --> 00:04:25.280
The first involved a comparison
of two strains of rats,

00:04:25.280 --> 00:04:28.520
one bred to have a high
intrinsic running capacity

00:04:28.520 --> 00:04:31.760
and the other bred to have
a low running capacity.

00:04:31.760 --> 00:04:34.360
Then they were randomized
to either get a running wheel

00:04:34.360 --> 00:04:35.760
or not.

00:04:35.760 --> 00:04:39.040
Even without exercising,
the high-fitness ability rats

00:04:39.040 --> 00:04:42.000
lived longer than those
with the low fitness ability,

00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:45.160
but unexpectedly the provision
of running wheels

00:04:45.160 --> 00:04:47.480
so they could all exercise
voluntarily

00:04:47.480 --> 00:04:50.400
cut the lives
of both strains short.

00:04:50.400 --> 00:04:53.920
Running reduced
their life expectancy.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.360
Using twin studies,
we can show that there are also

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:00.920
genetic predispositions
to exercise in humans too.

00:05:00.920 --> 00:05:04.360
When identical twins leave home
to start their separate lives,

00:05:04.360 --> 00:05:08.200
their exercise habits are much
more likely to be "concordant"

00:05:08.200 --> 00:05:09.540
than fraternal twins.

00:05:09.540 --> 00:05:12.200
This means if one twin
vigorously exercises,

00:05:12.200 --> 00:05:13.560
then the other twin
is more likely

00:05:13.560 --> 00:05:16.000
to do the same
if they share 100 percent

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:18.840
of their DNA instead
of just 50 percent

00:05:18.840 --> 00:05:21.840
like regular brothers
and sisters.

00:05:21.840 --> 00:05:25.560
How could we tell if it's
this genetic predisposition

00:05:25.560 --> 00:05:28.320
to exercise rather than
the actual exercise

00:05:28.320 --> 00:05:31.200
that accounts
for athletic longevity?

00:05:31.200 --> 00:05:35.240
Well, what about the rare cases
of identical twins

00:05:35.240 --> 00:05:38.600
whose exercise habits diverge?

00:05:38.600 --> 00:05:41.680
With the same DNA,
would intense physical activity

00:05:41.680 --> 00:05:43.360
make a difference?

00:05:43.360 --> 00:05:45.160
Apparently not.

00:05:45.160 --> 00:05:48.480
The same mortality rates
were found in identical twins

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:52.160
whether they
exercised vigorously or not.

00:05:52.160 --> 00:05:55.560
A critical analysis on whether
physical activity is a cause

00:05:55.560 --> 00:05:59.760
of longevity concluded
"the undisputed health-related

00:05:59.760 --> 00:06:02.520
benefits of exercise have yet
to translate into

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:07.040
any proven causal relationship
with longevity."

00:06:07.040 --> 00:06:09.520
This was based on the fact
that the randomized controlled

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:12.200
trials in clinical populations,
suffering mostly

00:06:12.200 --> 00:06:15.200
from neurological conditions,
have shown a drop

00:06:15.200 --> 00:06:16.960
in premature death rates.

00:06:16.960 --> 00:06:18.760
Exercise interventions
have failed

00:06:18.760 --> 00:06:20.400
to show exercise extends life

00:06:20.400 --> 00:06:23.240
among apparently healthy
individuals.

00:06:23.240 --> 00:06:26.800
However, these
are difficult studies to run.

00:06:26.800 --> 00:06:30.720
Take the largest such study,
where more than 1,500 older men

00:06:30.720 --> 00:06:33.720
and women (average age 73)
were randomized to one

00:06:33.720 --> 00:06:36.280
of three groups for five years.

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:39.200
A recommendation to just follow
the national guidelines

00:06:39.200 --> 00:06:41.360
of 30 minutes a day
five days a week,

00:06:41.360 --> 00:06:43.960
or to replace two of those
weekly sessions

00:06:43.960 --> 00:06:46.840
with 50-minute sessions,
or to replace two

00:06:46.840 --> 00:06:49.360
of those weekly sessions
with four four-minute bursts

00:06:49.360 --> 00:06:51.120
of high intensity
interval training

00:06:51.120 --> 00:06:53.560
at 90 percent
of peak heart rate.

00:06:53.560 --> 00:06:57.800
And there ended up being
no difference in mortality rates

00:06:57.800 --> 00:07:01.920
between the two extra exercise
groups and the control group.

00:07:01.920 --> 00:07:03.840
This may be because
the control group

00:07:03.840 --> 00:07:06.400
ended up exercising so much.

00:07:06.400 --> 00:07:09.640
The control group advised
to exercise for just 30 minutes

00:07:09.640 --> 00:07:12.840
ended up exercising
more than the group told

00:07:12.840 --> 00:07:16.440
to boost weekly sessions
up to 50 minutes.

00:07:16.440 --> 00:07:18.720
All participants had
the potential to be randomized

00:07:18.720 --> 00:07:21.120
to the high-intensity group,
so the enrollees

00:07:21.120 --> 00:07:23.640
had to be pretty fit
at baseline.

00:07:23.640 --> 00:07:26.520
And with expectations
of health checks at years one,

00:07:26.520 --> 00:07:28.200
three, and five,
the control group

00:07:28.200 --> 00:07:31.440
stepped it up so much
it blurred the distinctions

00:07:31.440 --> 00:07:35.000
between the three groups.

00:07:35.000 --> 00:07:36.040
In our final story,

00:07:36.040 --> 00:07:39.800
we look at the data that shows it may be
prudent to limit chronic, vigorous exercise

00:07:39.800 --> 00:07:41.780
to no more than an hour a day

00:07:41.780 --> 00:07:45.120
and no more than five hours a week.

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:48.720
How much exercise is too much?

00:07:48.720 --> 00:07:50.120
There don't appear
to be any studies

00:07:50.120 --> 00:07:53.480
on the longevity of marathon
or triathlon runners,

00:07:53.480 --> 00:07:55.720
but this study in which more
than a thousand joggers

00:07:55.720 --> 00:07:57.440
were followed
for up to a dozen years

00:07:57.440 --> 00:07:59.600
is able to offer some insight.

00:07:59.600 --> 00:08:02.800
Surprisingly,
the "light" joggers appeared

00:08:02.800 --> 00:08:05.000
to have the lowest
mortality rates.

00:08:05.000 --> 00:08:07.280
Moderate joggers
had a worse mortality rate

00:08:07.280 --> 00:08:08.840
than light joggers.

00:08:08.840 --> 00:08:11.880
They still did better than
that of sedentary nonjoggers,

00:08:11.880 --> 00:08:14.880
but strenuous joggers did not.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:16.280
There wasn't
a significant difference

00:08:16.280 --> 00:08:18.960
in death rates between those
jogging more than four hours

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.080
a week and those
not jogging at all.

00:08:21.080 --> 00:08:24.520
So, the death versus jogging
intensity graph

00:08:24.520 --> 00:08:27.480
was a U-shaped curve,
with the lowest mortality

00:08:27.480 --> 00:08:30.720
bottoming out among those
taking it relatively easy,

00:08:30.720 --> 00:08:34.600
clocking one to 2.4 hours a week
over no more than three days

00:08:34.600 --> 00:08:36.760
at a slow or average pace.

00:08:36.760 --> 00:08:40.240
So more may not necessarily
be better.

00:08:40.240 --> 00:08:43.560
The jogging study spurred
sensationalist headlines

00:08:43.560 --> 00:08:47.000
like "Fast Running Can Kill,"
but the strenuous joggers

00:08:47.000 --> 00:08:49.720
were just dying at a higher rate
compared to light joggers,

00:08:49.720 --> 00:08:51.720
not compared to
the general population

00:08:51.720 --> 00:08:53.280
of nonjoggers.

00:08:53.280 --> 00:08:55.200
Here's what
a meta-analysis found

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:57.400
of all such similar studies:

00:08:57.400 --> 00:09:00.360
Yes, diminishing returns,
but the only clear

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:04.800
mortality bump
was associated with inactivity.

00:09:04.800 --> 00:09:06.720
However, there was
a retrospective analysis

00:09:06.720 --> 00:09:09.560
of more than a thousand male
Olympic track and field athletes

00:09:09.560 --> 00:09:11.960
that suggested that those
who had extraordinary

00:09:11.960 --> 00:09:14.600
early peak performance
had lifespans shortened

00:09:14.600 --> 00:09:16.720
by almost five years.

00:09:16.720 --> 00:09:20.480
The regimen for peak performance
may not be the same as that

00:09:20.480 --> 00:09:23.280
for optimum health
and longevity.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.720
Perhaps the mantra
"exercise is medicine" metaphor

00:09:26.720 --> 00:09:31.360
should be taken a step further,
recognizing that like any

00:09:31.360 --> 00:09:35.383
powerful medicine there
may be a safe range of dosing.

00:09:35.383 --> 00:09:37.240
